---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import PersonalizedAdsPrivacy from '../../components/PersonalizedAdsPrivacy.astro';
import { rel, SUPPORT_EMAIL, LEGAL_NAME } from '../../consts';
const description = `Privacy policy for Table Talk: Conversation Cards by ${LEGAL_NAME}.`;
---
<Base title="Privacy Policy · Table Talk: Conversation Cards" description={description} path="/privacy/table-talk">
  <main class="page">
    <div class="wrap" style="max-width:720px;">
      <a class="back" href={rel('/privacy/')}>← All privacy policies</a>
      <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
      <p class="updated">Table Talk: Conversation Cards · Effective September 15, 2026</p>

      <h2>The short version</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Table Talk is a free conversation-card app, paid for by ads and optional in-app purchases. There are no accounts. Every card is bundled in the app, and your bookmarks stay on your device.</li>
        <li>When you first open the app, Apple asks two questions: may the app track you, and may it send notifications. Tapping “Allow” on tracking allows <strong>personalized ads</strong> and turns on anonymous <strong>usage analytics</strong> and <strong>crash reports</strong>. Otherwise ads are non-personalized, and analytics and crash reports stay off unless you turn them on.</li>
        <li><strong>No ads at all in the EEA, the UK or Switzerland.</strong></li>
        <li>We don't sell your data for money. Some US state laws count personalized ads as “selling” or “sharing”, and you can <a href="#us-state-privacy-rights">opt out at any time</a> by turning tracking off.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What changed on September 15, 2026</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Ads can now be personalized, but only for people who tap “Allow” on Apple's tracking prompt. Everyone else gets non-personalized ads.</li>
        <li>Besides Google AdMob, ads can now come from Meta Audience Network. Other partner networks listed below may be added later.</li>
        <li>This policy now states that there are no ads in the EEA, the UK or Switzerland, and that ads are non-personalized when the app can't read your region.</li>
        <li>New section on US state privacy rights (California, Virginia, Colorado, Connecticut, Utah, Texas, Oregon and others), including how to opt out.</li>
        <li>The earlier version was out of date. This one now covers usage analytics (PostHog), crash reports (Sentry), the weekly reminder and in-app purchases.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Versions of the app released before this date request non-personalized ads only.</p>

      <h2>Your choices at first launch</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Tracking (Apple's prompt).</strong> “Allow” allows personalized ads and turns on usage analytics and crash reports. “Ask App Not to Track” keeps ads non-personalized and leaves analytics and crash reports off.</li>
        <li><strong>Notifications (Apple's prompt).</strong> “Allow” turns on one optional weekly reminder. “Don't Allow” leaves it off.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        You can change all of this later. Table Talk's <strong>Settings → Privacy</strong> has switches for
        <strong>Usage analytics</strong> and <strong>Crash reports</strong>, and <strong>Settings → Reminders</strong>
        controls the reminder. To change tracking, see <a href="#advertising">Advertising</a> below.
      </p>

      <h2>What stays on your device</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Your bookmarks, your “Shuffle My Picks” deck and your recent card history.</li>
        <li>Your settings, reminder schedule, and purchase and ads state.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>We don't receive any of this. Deleting the app removes it from your device.</p>

      <h2>Prompts and sharing</h2>
      <p>
        All prompt text is included in the app. When you share a card, Table Talk makes an image on your
        device and hands it to the standard iOS share sheet. The destination you choose, such as Messages or a social
        app, handles it under its own privacy practices.
      </p>

      <h2>Usage analytics (PostHog)</h2>
      <p>
        When usage analytics is on, the app sends simple usage events to <strong>PostHog</strong>, our analytics
        provider. Examples are that a card session started or finished, or which screen you opened. Each event
        comes with a random ID created for this install, the app version, device model and iOS version. We don't
        create a profile of you, and we never send your name, email, contacts or anything you type. Like any
        internet service, PostHog receives your IP address when the app connects, and it may use that to estimate
        a rough location. Analytics is never used for advertising. When you turn the switch off, collection stops. See
        <a href="https://posthog.com/privacy" rel="noopener">PostHog's privacy policy</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Crash reports (Sentry)</h2>
      <p>
        When crash reports are on, a crash or serious error sends a technical report to <strong>Sentry</strong>. It
        contains what went wrong in the code, the app version, device model and iOS version, so we can fix it. Crash
        reports don't include screenshots, your name or contact details, and Sentry's option for collecting
        personal information is turned off. See <a href="https://sentry.io/privacy/" rel="noopener">Sentry's privacy policy</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Weekly reminder</h2>
      <p>
        If you allow notifications, Table Talk schedules one local reminder a week (“Table Talk night”) with a
        conversation starter from the free deck. It's scheduled on your device, with no push server and no
        marketing. You can change the day and time or turn it off in <strong>Settings → Reminders</strong>.
      </p>

      <PersonalizedAdsPrivacy
        appName="Table Talk"
        placements="The free version shows banner ads on Home and during a card session. It may also show a full-screen ad when you leave a longer card session and return to Home, never in the middle of a card."
        adFreeOptions="Buying Remove Ads"
      />

      <h2>Purchases</h2>
      <p>
        Table Talk offers optional in-app purchases: Remove Ads, three prompt packs, a pack bundle and the Daily
        Featured Archive. Apple processes them. We never see or store your payment details, and Apple handles billing under
        <a href="https://www.apple.com/legal/privacy/">Apple's privacy policy</a>. Purchases can be restored on any
        device signed in to the same Apple Account from <strong>Settings → Restore Purchases</strong>.
      </p>

      <h2>How long data is kept</h2>
      <p>
        Data on your device stays until you delete the app. Analytics and crash data are kept only as long as they
        help us improve the app, then deleted under our PostHog and Sentry retention settings. We don't combine them
        with data from other sources or from our other apps.
      </p>

      <h2>Children</h2>
      <p>
        Table Talk is a general-audience app. Some packs are meant for families to play together, but the app isn't
        directed at children, and we don't knowingly collect personal information from children under 13. If you
        think a child has sent us personal information, email us and we'll delete it.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes</h2>
      <p>
        If this policy changes materially, the new version will be posted at this URL with a new effective date and
        a summary of what changed.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>{LEGAL_NAME}, Colorado, USA. Questions or privacy requests: <a href={`mailto:${SUPPORT_EMAIL}`}>{SUPPORT_EMAIL}</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </main>
</Base>
